# FreeSail: the captain's orders for the passage of gate 5b in the schooner (spec M5 §15,
# §18, §20; packages 33a and 34). The frigate's book (gate-5b-passage.orders) to the outer
# road, word for word; from there the schooner, a small vessel with a pilot's knowledge of
# the harbour in her master, runs in by the eastern channel and comes to an anchor off the
# town in Carrick Road (Imray 1874, p. 90: "between St Mawes and Falmouth banks, 7 to 17
# fathoms, the usual anchorage for ships of war"; White 1835, p. 27). The frigate, deep
# and without a pilot, anchors in the outer road.

# The departure: while the land is in sight astern, a bearing of it every glass, so that
# the account is fixed off the Stiff before the land is lost (N §1).
# Package 37d: a bearing gives a line, and the lookout's distance by estimation is laid
# down with it only when it is the better figure (a landfall on one mark after a long
# run), so the book takes a fix by cross bearings after the bearing, which stands when
# one mark only is in sight or the marks cut too fine.
standing order "bearings": every glass, if the land is in sight then take a bearing of the land; take a fix

# The Channel Soundings: at noon she is brought to and the deep-sea lead hove (Falconer
# 1780, 'Sounding'; Rennell's procedure, N §3).
standing order "bring to for soundings": at noon then heave to
standing order "soundings": when the speed is under 3 knots and the distance run since noon is under 5 miles then heave the deep-sea lead

# Once the cast is had she fills away, and when the fill-away has left the helm the
# course for Falmouth is shaped afresh from the account.
standing order "fill away after the cast": at a sounding, if the distance run since noon is under 5 miles then fill away
standing order "stand on": at filled away then shape a course for 50 02 N 4 58 W

# The landfall: the first land seen gives a bearing, and the course for Falmouth is
# shaped from the reckoning.
standing order "landfall": when the land is in sight then take a bearing of the land; shape a course for 50 02 N 4 58 W

# Package 37e: a course shaped is now a course made good, the master's tide allowed in
# it (spec M5 §15), and the order's own words say what the line does: from the Lizard's
# landfall the line for Falmouth "crosses the land about Black Head" and "passes the
# Manacles within a mile". Until 37e the heading for Falmouth was held and the flood set
# her a league to the eastward of that line, clear of the ledge; made good, the line is
# sailed and she struck. So the book rounds the Manacles as the merchant passage's does,
# by a point three miles east of them (50 02 N 4 58 W), and shapes for Falmouth when it
# is within a mile by the account, which the bearings and the fix hold to a few cables.
standing order "round the Manacles": when the distance to 50 02 N 4 58 W is under 1 mile then shape a course for Falmouth

# The pilot (package 37e): the Falmouth cutter hails her as she stands in, and until 37e
# the pilot boarded a minute after. From the point east of the Manacles she stands in NNW
# at seven knots and he is left astern, hailed and not aboard. Three mends were tried and
# none is kept: sail shortened at his hail, and the fore topsail taken in, each put her
# on the ground within St Anthony's (she ran the entrance slower, on the ebb); brought to
# for his boat she lay to two glasses and the cutter did not come alongside. For the
# lead: `docs/dev/TuningNotes.md`, package 37e.

# Running in: the hand lead every glass once the land is in sight and the run since
# noon says she is closing the coast, and every ten minutes within the thirty-five
# miles since noon that put her off the Manacles (Luce 1884 ch. XXV).
standing order "lead going": every glass, if the land is in sight and the distance run since noon exceeds 20 miles then heave the lead
standing order "lead going in": every 10 minutes, if the land is in sight and the distance run since noon exceeds 35 miles then heave the lead

# At the first cast under twenty fathoms she is in the outer road; a small vessel with
# her master's knowledge of the harbour shortens sail and stands on for Carrick Road by
# the eastern channel (White 1835, p. 26: "the eastern channel is the better"), the lead
# going every ten minutes, and comes to an anchor off the town at the first cast under
# eight fathoms, which is the harbour's mouth within St Anthony's: in Luce's manner (ch.
# XXXIV, 'Beating in on a flood tide, to anchor', p. 573: head to the stronger of wind
# and tide), the best bower let go with the way off her and the cable veered to five
# times the depth (package 34; spec M5 §18). The lead, 2026-10-01: shaped for the Road
# under all plain sail she ran the eastern channel at eight knots and struck the Lugo
# rock off St Mawes castle before the lead could say the Road's depth; sail shortened,
# and the anchor at the eight-fathom line, she brings up in the mouth.
standing order "the outer road": when the depth is under 20 fathoms then shorten sail; shape a course for Carrick Road
standing order "off the town": when the depth is under 10 fathoms then come to an anchor
